📌 Suppose You Have This Composite Index
(created_date, role_type, other_col)

Index order matters.

✅ Case 1 — All Equal Conditions (Best Case)
WHERE created_date = '2025-09-12'
  AND role_type = 'ADMIN'

👉 Database can use:

created_date

then role_type

This is perfect index usage.

❗ Case 2 — Range Condition Appears  (<, > or between)
WHERE created_date > '2025-09-01'
  AND role_type = 'ADMIN'

Now something changes.

🔎 What Happens Internally?

Indexes are stored like a sorted tree (B-Tree).

Think of it like this:

2025-09-01, ADMIN
2025-09-01, USER
2025-09-02, ADMIN
2025-09-02, USER
2025-09-03, ADMIN
...

When you write:
created_date > '2025-09-01'

Database must:

Find first matching date

Then scan forward for all greater dates

⚠️ Once it starts scanning a range,
it cannot efficiently jump to role_type = 'ADMIN'.

It must check role_type row by row.

🚨 Important Rule

In composite index, once a RANGE condition is used, columns after it cannot be used for index search (only filtering).

🎯 So In This Query
WHERE created_date > '2025-09-01'
  AND role_type = 'ADMIN'

Index usage:

| Column       | Used For           |
| ------------ | ------------------ |
| created_date | ✅ Index search     |
| role_type    | ❌ Post-filter only |

🔥 Why?

Because index works like:

(created_date → then role_type → then other_col)

But range breaks the strict ordering.

Equal condition = precise lookup
Range condition = scanning starts

After scanning starts → no more precise narrowing.

📌 Interview-Ready Statement

In a composite index, columns after a range condition cannot be used for index lookup — they are only used for filtering.

